WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:06.550
OK.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.630
Let's get started.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.180
So welcome everyone.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.980
So last time we talked about
information and rate distortion.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.050
And the first
problem in the quiz

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.360
is just a straightforward
application

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.130
of the formulae we saw in class.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.260
So the first question was.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.570
You have been given some
joint distribution table

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.520
and you are asked to
compute the joint entropy

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.140
and mutual information.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.280
So just go over this.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.850
It's straightforward
calculation, nothing tricky,

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.600
just so that you start
feeling comfortable

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.990
with these constructs.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.690
And then second question
was now basically

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.610
you had a uniformly
distributed source

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.010
on a discrete alphabet, 0, 1, 2.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.430
And then we saw in class the
rate distortion function.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:52.120
So the distortion we are working
under is mean square error.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.820
And then you were asked
this RD of RD equals to 0.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.990
This you could have--
you really need RD.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.410
D equals to 0 is just
calculation of entropy,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:04.250
so straightforward.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.520
And then the second question
was, what is RD equals to 1?

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.580
And again, actually you
didn't need any calculation

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.820
for this part because if
you take mean square error

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.970
distortion of 1,
you could actually

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.340
always transmit symbol 1.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.910
And for any given symbol
then, your mean square error

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:24.980
or just square error
between that symbol

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.640
and the reconstruction
is always bounded by 1.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.430
So if you always transmit
symbol 1, you are good.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.350
And that basically
means you don't really

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.150
need to send anything.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.630
The reconstructor can
just always recover 1.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.830
And that will basically
lead to a distortion of 1

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.900
and hence your rate is 0.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.990
So this is just so that
you guys, again, understand

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.280
what's really happening.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.550
You don't have to always
do the complicated maths.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.785
This is just so that you
understand the concept.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.050
And finally, I
think-- yeah, so this

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.570
was the last question
where now we were working

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:03.920
with Bernoulli 1/2 source.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.670
And we had Hamming, distortion
which we saw in class.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.919
So Hamming distortion
is just distortion is 1

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:13.880
if symbols don't match, and
it's 0 if symbols match.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.390
And for this source, we saw--
so Saki worked this out in class

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.490
that your rate distortion
function turns out

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:24.890
to be 1 minus the entropy
at probability distortion D.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.490
And then we ask, which of
the following is correct?

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:32.060
And so again, it's a quite
straightforward thing.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.700
It's just so that you understand
what the rate distortion

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:35.900
statement says.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.630
And so what it says is
that basically there

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:43.160
exists a scheme which when
working on large block sizes,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.600
which achieves distortion
D, and your rate always

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.820
has to be greater than this RD.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.760
You can't compress
more than this RD.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.530
And so first one is wrong.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.900
You can't actually get
rate more than this.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.280
Second one is correct.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.650
You can get rate
greater than this.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.420
And actually what
Saki talked about is

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.302
we didn't show the
proof, but this

00:03:05.302 --> 00:03:06.510
is a much stronger statement.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.780
This is exactly analogous
to the entropy case

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.112
where you can actually
get arbitrary close to--

00:03:15.112 --> 00:03:17.070
If you allow me to take
larger and larger block

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.445
sizes, larger and
larger symbols,

00:03:18.445 --> 00:03:20.440
I can get arbitrarily
close to this.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.340
So the third option
was also correct.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.280
And finally what's really
important is this large block

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:26.940
sizes.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.150
So this allowed us to do
this asymptotic analysis.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.880
So if you recall from last
lecture, Saki, for example,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.350
even for Gaussians where he had
the sphere packing argument,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.510
it was contingent on the
fact that you're working

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.740
on really large dimension.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.740
That's when you will
have minimal overlaps.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.110
And so this part is
really important.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.060
And again, it's very
similar to entropy.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.400
Entropy doesn't say you
can do it symbol by symbol.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.470
But there exists large
enough block sizes

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.020
where you can achieve that.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.920
So this is actually untrue,
that working on single symbols,

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.890
there is no guarantee that
you can actually achieve this.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.070
Any questions on the quiz?

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:09.900
Good.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.660
So let's just quickly
recap what we covered--

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:14.160
sorry.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.260
I keep clicking this--
and then continue on to.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.600
So today's lecture,
we'll be building

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.850
on what we learned in
last class and then

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:22.560
learning more about
transform coding,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.570
which is a new technique.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.190
So quick recap.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.760
So last time we first learned
about mutual information.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.630
So information [AUDIO OUT]
X, Y, two variables.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:39.710
And let's say I have joint
distribution px of y [AUDIO OUT]

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:42.020
mutual information.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.750
And you calculated mutual
information using this.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.700
There are various other versions
of the mutual information

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.930
formula, but this is
the basic definition.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.600
Then we learned a bit about
Shannon's rate distortion

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:57.290
theory.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:59.690
And again in Shannon's
rate distortion theory,

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.540
assume that you have
x1 to xn symbols, which

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.500
is some data which
is generated IID.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.820
Then the optimal rate, R of D,
for a given maximum distortion

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.210
D, so this is the best you can
do, the minimal rate you can get

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:20.450
for that distortion is given by
the minimal mutual information

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:24.440
over expected value of
distortions xy less than

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.740
or equal to D.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.790
And just in case, it was not
very clear in the last lecture,

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:35.330
your expectation is minimum
over all distributions

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:38.750
x, comma y, all joint
distributions x, comma y,

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:40.190
which you can also
think of being

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:45.500
factorized at px qy given
x because this term is

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:46.550
given to you.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:47.960
The px is given to.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:50.180
You have some source which
follows some probability

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:51.050
distribution.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.700
And so what you are
doing is you are

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.610
allowed to come up with any
constructions of y, which

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.760
minimizes this mutual
information to give you

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:03.130
the optimal rate distortion.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.080
And another important
thing to note

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:09.400
here was that we were
working with IID data.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.430
And a distortion really was a
symbol by symbol distortion.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:16.750
So let's say xn is a
n tuple of symbols.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:20.470
So this we approximated
it-- not approximated--

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:24.700
we assume that I can calculate
my distortion symbol by symbol.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.868
So if I have an n tuple,
so this is just x1,

00:06:27.868 --> 00:06:31.810
x2, xn and similarly y1, y2, yn.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:35.050
That my distortion is
basically per tuple sum,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.600
so another small point
to be careful about.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.130
And then we finally worked out--
we actually worked out also

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.340
Bernoulli source under
Hamming distortion.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.190
But something which we are
going to use in this class

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.220
repeatedly to drive
home some points is

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.550
this Gaussian source under
mean square error distortion.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:07:00.210
So, again, whenever we are
talking about RD, be careful

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.520
about the source
distribution, obviously, that

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.210
determines the how well you can
do, but also the distortion.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:07.230
So it's not clear.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.510
Always ask this question
if somebody comes and tells

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:12.450
you, what's the rate distortion,
do ask under what distortion

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.235
are we working.

00:07:13.235 --> 00:07:14.610
Or if somebody
comes and asks you

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.870
to design a lossy compressor, do
ask, what's the right distortion

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.150
to work with for that
particular application,

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.980
that particular source.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:26.190
So specifically, again, for
Gaussian and mean squared error

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:32.130
distortion, we saw that if X
is some normal 0, comma sigma

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.920
square, you had some--

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:35.982
you have some Gaussian.

00:07:35.982 --> 00:07:37.440
So actually this
shouldn't be unit.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.220
So in class, we saw the case
for X normal 0, comma 1.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.010
So I'm just generalizing it
to now normal 0, comma sigma

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:45.640
square.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:49.450
So you can work out exactly
similarly just variance scaling

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:51.310
[INAUDIBLE] sigma square.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.890
And our symbol by symbol
distortion was this.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:54.950
Just mean square error.

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.520
So you're going to
square the differences.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.830
We saw that the rate is
given by 1/2 log sigma square

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.340
by D if your D is between
0 and sigma square.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:11.020
And if your D is greater than
sigma square, your rate is 0.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.310
So this is really important.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:15.670
So what this is saying in this
Gaussian case, think about it,

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.400
is that if you're allowed
very large distortion,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.960
if you're allowed
it to be larger

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.630
than the variance
in your symbols,

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.600
then you don't actually
need to transmit anything

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.610
because whatever the
mean thing you recover,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:30.550
so we worked out the maths
earlier expected value of x

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:34.210
given y in this particular
case, whatever the single symbol

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.330
you are going to recover,
it's basically guaranteed

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.880
to have the mean square
error always less than D.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:42.580
It's actually always guaranteed
to have the mean square error

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:43.360
sigma square.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.950
That's what it means.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:49.310
So in this particular case
if I always transmit 0,

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:53.300
I know that my expected value
of x minus 0 whole square

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:57.500
is actually sigma square,
which is always less than D.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:58.880
So I can always transmit zero.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:01.730
I actually don't have
to send over anything.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:05.300
But if my distortion, maximum
distortion which I'm allowed

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:08.660
is less than sigma
square, then I

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.690
have to be intelligent
in selecting my codebook.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.490
And what you can
show is that when

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.030
you do that optimally,
again, using one intuition

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:21.230
was that fair packing argument,
that beautiful high dimensions

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.510
fair packing argument
which Saki gave,

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.570
you can show that the minimal
rate you can get is this term,

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:32.100
1/2 log sigma square by D.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.580
So another way to
think about it is

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:40.400
if your source
have low variance,

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.535
you can actually get away
with much fewer bits.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.010
Does that make sense?

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:50.800
So if my sigma is really
low, I can get away

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.930
in life with lower
D because you are

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:55.820
saying my symbol is
very tightly bound,

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.610
so if you allow me to have
very large distortion,

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:01.050
obviously, I can just throw
away a lot of information

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:02.675
and just get away
with a single symbol.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.520
So this is just
another way to look

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.572
at this formula,
very simple formula,

00:10:09.572 --> 00:10:11.780
another intuition which you
are getting through that.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:20.520
Everyone with me?

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:25.520
This is really important, and
we'll see trivial extensions

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:28.340
to this which actually
results in, I would say,

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.040
not so trivial
practical implications.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.700
Good.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.610
So just another small thing,
just notation wise sometimes.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:43.190
We write this as this so,
Saki also mentioned this,

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:47.810
but just as a revision point
where you can write RD actually

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:53.990
as RX,D just to highlight that,
what's really your source.

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:57.800
And so for Gaussian, we might
write it as R subscript of G

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:00.440
just to say Gaussian and just
directly say the variance which

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:04.400
just means mean 0
and distortion D.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.977
And there's this
another notation

00:11:06.977 --> 00:11:08.060
which you can write it as.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:11.120
So you can see that if D is
greater than sigma square,

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.150
then your sigma square
by D will be less than 1.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:16.760
And this whole term
would be negative.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:20.540
So another way to think about
this plus is just max of 0,

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:26.000
comma X. So this is
how we define x plus.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.420
So this is just another way
to write these are equivalent.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.190
So if sigma square
is greater than D,

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.950
this quantity is
positive and you can just

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:37.490
write 1/2 log sigma square by D.
If sigma square is less than D,

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.160
this quantity is negative and we
are not taking negative value.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.910
We'll just flip it to 0.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:45.560
So just a very simple
mathematical notation.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:49.010
And finally this is what I
wanted to show you last class,

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:51.680
but we also saw it
the class before.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:55.130
So this is the
performance for, again,

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:58.670
the Gaussian source normal
0, comma sigma square.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.770
On the x-axis, I have the rate.

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:04.580
On the y-axis, I have the
mean square distortion.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:06.260
The red curve is--

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:09.860
so since my x-axis is R instead
of RD, I'm just plotting.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:16.490
So if my RD is 1/2 log sigma
square by D, then my D of R

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.910
is just 2 raised to minus 2R.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:23.680
So actually this is
for sigma equals to 1.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:24.970
So 1 second.

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:26.620
So my D of R would be--

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:33.580
2 raise to minus
2R by sigma square.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:41.380
So here I'm just
plotting it for sigma 1.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:44.980
The red curve is the theoretical
best which you can do.

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:47.050
And the blue points,
which you see here,

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:49.690
is with respect to-- so
GL is generalized Lloyd,

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:53.980
but you can think of k
means with k equals to 2.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.990
And this is really important.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:00.730
So what this plot says is this
red curve, which you see here,

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:01.450
correct--

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:04.540
so this red curve is the
theoretical best you can do.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:06.550
Come up with any
scheme in the world.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:10.030
Take as large block
sizes as you want.

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:10.810
Do your best.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:12.370
You might have to
do a lot of effort.

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:13.840
We saw in vector
quantization, it's

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.280
not trivial as you keep
increasing the block sizes.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.500
This red curve is what
you would have obtained.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:23.140
And with just doing
k-means with k equals to 2

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:24.640
and taking those
vector quantizer,

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:28.240
so just working with the
two dimensional vectors

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.040
for our codebook,
we can already get

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:33.760
a performance which maybe looks
like this or these blue points.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:38.990
So you can see you can,
depending on the application,

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:42.382
it maybe OK to just work
with this practical scheme.

00:13:42.382 --> 00:13:43.840
The overhead which
you are getting,

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:47.748
you might have to pay a lot of
costs in terms of complexity.

00:13:47.748 --> 00:13:49.290
And so even though
the scheme exists,

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:52.920
it's just to show you
comparative performance.

00:13:58.930 --> 00:13:59.550
I'm sorry.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:01.320
This is my bad.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:02.040
This is wrong.

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:06.210
Obviously, this should be
this because sigma square by D

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:07.165
is 2 raised to 2R.

00:14:07.165 --> 00:14:07.665
Sorry.

00:14:15.172 --> 00:14:17.070
I'm going to
reiterate this point.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:18.610
It's going to come
again and again.

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:20.640
So one thumb rule
so far which we

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:23.120
saw about lossy
compression [AUDIO OUT]

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:28.380
Gaussian case is that basically
for a given distortion,

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:31.170
you would like to allocate
more bits to components

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:33.960
with higher variance.

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:35.760
If your variance
is really low, you

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:42.330
can get away with giving
lower number of bits to it.

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:42.830
Correct.

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:46.220
This is just like what
we discussed here.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:50.480
This is one way to think about
what we saw, one intuitive

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.280
take away.

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:56.030
And thus does it make sense to
everyone even just intuitively?

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:59.690
If you have higher variance,
you need more bits,

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.640
more codebook points
to cover so that you

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.290
have some constant distortion.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:05.120
Whereas if you have
lower variance,

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:06.680
some data is spread less.

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:11.940
You can get away with fewer
points for same distortion.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:15.400
I think you meant
distortion level.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:17.320
Yeah, distortion level, sorry.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.260
Thank you.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:22.210
This should be level.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:22.780
Thank you.

00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.800
So far everyone with me?

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:32.300
OK.

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.130
So then today we'll
learn a bit more

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:36.470
about this
water-filling intuition

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:38.300
for correlated Gaussian sources.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:42.170
So far we have been working
with these IID sources.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.780
We are not going to look at
a general correlated source

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:47.540
just yet, but this is going to
give you some intuition on again

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:48.410
what you can do.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:52.520
And then we'll use this
intuition to learn, to motivate,

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:58.010
I would say, transform coding
which is just, I would say,

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:00.800
it's omnipresent in all
lossy compression techniques

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:03.560
in some way or the other.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:04.910
So let's get started.

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:08.690
So again, your lossy
compression framework,

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.850
which we have been
seeing is this.

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:14.180
So you have some source
symbols X1 to Xk.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:18.350
You encode it into some
encode codebook vectors.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:21.890
And so your rate
is log N by K. We

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:24.980
have seen this bits per symbol.

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:26.360
You get some bits.

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:28.850
You transmit this
bits to the decoder

00:16:28.850 --> 00:16:31.460
and that recover
these hat symbols.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:33.410
And so the two terms
for lossy compression

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:36.170
really is the rate because
you need log N bits.

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:39.110
And you use them to
transmit K symbols.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:42.170
So it's log N by K bits
per source component,

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:46.280
and then your distortion between
these two, what you recovered

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:48.830
and what was supposed
to be encoded.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:53.510
And as we saw, we are working
with memoryless sources or IID

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:54.050
sources.

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:58.760
So we can just easily break it
down into individual components.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:06.079
So last time, the Shannon's
theorem which we saw.

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:10.810
It assumed that your X1 to
X2 is IID data generated.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.270
And in that case, we
saw that optimal rate RD

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:14.720
is given by this.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:16.577
If you, again, I
want to reiterate,

00:17:16.577 --> 00:17:18.369
it's really beautiful
that we could come up

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:19.569
with something close form.

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:23.440
In this case, this is optimize
over all possible schemes

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:25.030
in the world which
you could have done

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.410
for this particular source.

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:27.550
And that gives you--

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:31.210
this is a very nice
formulation, mutual information.

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.670
And we got this.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:37.760
But in reality, your
data is not IID.

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.650
In reality, your
data is correlated.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.950
So what can you do here?

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.610
And actually you can show that
you can generalize the Shannon.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:50.090
So next few statements are
going to be without proofs.

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:52.970
And if you are interested in
the proof, please take E376,

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:56.930
which goes into much more depth
about all of these things.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:18:00.110
But now let's say you
have some source block Xn,

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:03.440
so again, this is what we
have been looking at, just

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:09.550
X1 to Xn symbols and your
reconstruction X hat n.

00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:12.460
Then you can show that
your rate for X and D

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.850
is actually given by
this, again, formula.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:20.620
And here the mutual information
is between symbols Xn and X hat

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:22.610
n.

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:25.370
And now you have not
assumed anything about Xns.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:29.150
You haven't assumed that
Xn factorizes as IID,

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:32.180
the probabilities are
going to be just multiples.

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:33.800
It can be anything.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:36.290
And remember this
whole minimization

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:38.450
was under the
joint distribution.

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:42.650
So here this minimization would
be under joint distribution

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.440
of PXn, PX hat n.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:54.280
And it wasn't obvious
that the Shannon

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.280
theorem will generalize for this
particular case but it does.

00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:02.370
And one way to think about
this is just your RX,

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:05.250
comma D was the
analog for entropy

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:09.810
in the lossless case
for X. RXn, comma D,

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.510
you can think of it as an analog
for the entropy of the n-tuple.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:20.130
So this is just generalization,
going beyond IID.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:28.080
And actually, there is
another level of extension.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.510
So now if you
assume that you have

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:33.000
a stationary stochastic
process, which

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.180
I'm going to denote
by this boldface X,

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:40.140
so you have X1, X2,
X3, Shubham talked

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:42.990
about stationary
stochastic process in the--

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:45.630
I think, in lecture 8.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:51.070
In that case, what you can show
that is there is a quantity rate

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:54.610
X, comma D where X is now
your stochastic process.

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:58.240
And this can be calculated
by taking the limit n tends

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.120
to infinity of this tuple Xn.

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:07.510
So this RXn, comma D is
just the rate distortion

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:08.410
for the tuple Xn.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:16.180
Again, if we have
to bring analogs,

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:20.740
this RX,D is the analog for the
entropy rate of the n-tuple.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:25.060
So we talked about entropy rate,
and we showed-- we asked that.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:27.880
We took that limit n
tends to infinity there.

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:30.190
Similarly, we can--
we are basically

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:31.960
doing the exact same
parallels like what

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:33.340
we did in lossy compression.

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:36.220
And we show that
similar generalizations

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:39.820
exist for Shannon's
RD theorem as well.

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:45.850
And what you can show is that
this limit exists basically

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:46.900
for stationary sources.

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:51.153
Again, I'm being hand-wavy
but that's intentional.

00:20:51.153 --> 00:20:53.320
If you are really interested
into the maths of this,

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:56.710
please take E376 where we can
point you to specific resources

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:59.380
like how you can
show these things.

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:02.450
But the main takeaway
is really this,

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:06.670
which is the best you can
do for stationary processes.

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:10.180
In the limit of encoding
arbitrary many symbols

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:12.850
in a block, so we are not very
worried about the complexity

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:18.250
at all, is basically
this term RX, comma D,

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:19.810
the stationary process term.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:27.860
So I haven't really done
anything different so far.

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:29.720
Just what we have
shown is that we

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:32.750
could have used whatever we
have done with Shannon's theorem

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:34.670
for also correlated sources.

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:38.030
But now this term might be
hard to compute in practice

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:38.900
like we saw.

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:41.750
Even in entropy other
than simpler cases,

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.720
we didn't really do much.

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:48.280
But I think it is
interesting to look

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:52.720
at one specific example of
how you can use this theorem.

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:55.480
It has a lot of intuition.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:58.690
So first of all I'll pause for a
second and questions, everyone's

00:21:58.690 --> 00:21:59.200
with me.

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:05.270
So now let's work through
a very specific example.

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:07.040
Assume you have a
Gaussian source,

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:09.230
and we are going to work
with block sizes of 2.

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:12.948
So we are going to always
remember this picture, I think,

00:22:12.948 --> 00:22:14.240
when you are thinking of lossy.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:19.070
So you input K symbols in
a block into the encoder.

00:22:19.070 --> 00:22:23.370
And so here we are going
to work with K equals to 2.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:26.280
Oh, sorry, I think I
went ahead of myself.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:27.550
Yeah.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:28.050
OK.

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:31.470
So let's say your X1 is a
normal sigma 0, sigma 1 square.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.960
X2 is normal zero,
sigma 2 square.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:38.910
And let's assume they are
independent still for now.

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:41.790
And then the way we have
been looking at blocks,

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:44.010
X2 is just X1, X2.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.470
It's just a two
dimensional vector.

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.490
So what you're going
to do is at each--

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:51.240
one way to think about
is I'm going to go,

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:54.083
I'm going to sample X1 with
normal 0, comma sigma 1 square.

00:22:54.083 --> 00:22:55.500
I'm going to sample,
take a sample

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:58.740
from normal 0, comma sigma
2 square, combine them.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.247
I'm going to
lossily transmit it.

00:23:00.247 --> 00:23:01.830
Then I'm going to
repeat this process.

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:04.740
And I'm going to
keep doing this.

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:06.270
And this is just the notation.

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:10.140
So our notation now for
this X2, RX2, comma D

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:12.540
is I'm going to
call it G. And then

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:15.000
I'm just going to replace it
with sigma 1 square, sigma 2

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:16.980
square because
that's what matters.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:21.100
This is just generalization of
our single dimensional notation

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:24.610
comma D.

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:27.820
So in this case,
what you can actually

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:32.590
show is your rate
distortion for this

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:37.630
if you were doing in blocks
independent Gaussians,

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:39.460
you can show that
it turns out to be

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:46.170
minimum of half of
RG sigma 1 square D1

00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:48.390
plus RG sigma 1
square-- oh, sorry.

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:50.980
This should be sigma 2 square.

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:52.515
I can just fix it.

00:23:52.515 --> 00:23:55.190
It should be sigma 2 square--

00:23:55.190 --> 00:24:01.130
RG sigma 2 square comma
D2 and just 1/2 of it

00:24:01.130 --> 00:24:05.720
such that your D1 plus D2
average is less than D.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.780
So essentially
what you can show,

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:11.673
we're not going to
work this out but this

00:24:11.673 --> 00:24:13.340
I would say it's
almost straightforward.

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:14.790
We can we can talk about it.

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:23.640
What this really means is that
I can independently lossily

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:27.500
compress my source 1,
give it a distortion D1.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:32.700
I can independently
compress my source 2,

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:34.380
give it a distortion D2.

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:37.350
But now since I'm
doing them together,

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.980
my average distortion should
be less than or equal to D.

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:43.950
So in some sense, what
it's allowing me to do

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:47.190
is break my
distortion D. And it's

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:50.100
allowing me to give a distortion
D1 to source component 1

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:53.010
and a distortion D2 to
source component 2 such

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:59.190
that on average, my distortion
is still less than D.

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:03.840
And then it's basically
[AUDIO OUT] dependently optimize

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.340
over each channel.

00:25:06.340 --> 00:25:09.070
So the question really
is, what is D1, D2?

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:11.020
We'll figure that
out in a second.

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:13.540
What's the ideal
split I should give?

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:18.540
But this you can show
from almost [AUDIO OUT]

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:21.300
if you just, for example, expand
the mutual information out.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:24.640
So in this particular
case, you can just work out

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:25.660
the mutual information.

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:27.880
You know how we worked
it out for a 1D case.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:30.880
Similarly you can work
it out for the 2D case,

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:34.460
and you get this--

00:25:34.460 --> 00:25:37.310
you get this nice
looking formula.

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:39.470
And I think this
mistake, by the way,

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:43.563
might continue a couple more
slides, so just bear with me.

00:25:43.563 --> 00:25:45.230
If you're getting
confused, let me know.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:45.920
I'll fix it.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:54.820
And so this is just rewriting
our formula from previous slide.

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.690
And so we know what my RG
sigma 1 square, comma D1 is.

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:02.770
So that's just Gaussian with
variance sigma and given

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:03.820
distortion D1.

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:09.700
So again I think this is 2.

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:12.280
And so here it's just
straightforward substitution

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:13.615
of what we saw in last class.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:23.190
So this now becomes our D
problem, which we need to solve.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:27.990
So what we really need to
solve, rate for this source

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:31.620
given distortion D is basically
an optimization problem such

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:36.360
that my minimum over D1 plus D2
by 2 is less than or equal to D.

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:39.160
And this is my
objective function.

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:40.290
So it's very simple.

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:44.040
What we are saying is, find me
the right split of distortions

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.740
between source component
1, source component 2 such

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:49.740
that if I independently
encode them together,

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:51.990
I get the minimal rate.

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:56.190
So what's really
this optimal split?

00:26:56.190 --> 00:27:00.450
So you can-- if you have taken
a convex optimization class,

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:04.020
this is actually a quite a
straightforward minimization

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:05.880
problem to solve.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:08.910
You just solve it
using KKT conditions.

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:11.200
But let's just look into
the answer for intuitions.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:12.450
If you haven't taken, it's OK.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:14.825
We can we can discuss how
exactly you solve this problem.

00:27:14.825 --> 00:27:17.010
It's not the point
of this class.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:19.990
But trust me, it's
not hard to solve.

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:22.180
It's very standard techniques.

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:29.010
So let's talk about it.

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:32.070
So without loss of
generality, so WLOG is just

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:34.350
without loss of generality,
let us assume my sigma

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:37.950
1 is less than sigma 2,
the source I'm encoding.

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:40.170
And then this is--

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:42.540
I need to figure out how
much distortion should I

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:44.520
give to the first
source, which has

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:48.120
sigma 1 and the second
source, which has sigma 2.

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:55.530
So my [AUDIO OUT] of
distortion, which I'm giving--

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:57.240
the amount of
distortion which I'm

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.070
giving to the first
component to be greater

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:00.060
than sigma one square?

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:07.440
A few of your heads going
in various directions,

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:09.645
but I would like to
see maybe a few more.

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:15.990
Is the question clear to
everyone, first of all,

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:17.310
what we are trying to do?

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.330
Would anyone like to answer.

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:28.850
OK.

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.500
Oh, if it's greater than sigma
1 square or if it's equal to,

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:34.240
that left term is going to be 0.

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:37.240
If it's greater than zero,
so then all you're doing

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:40.240
is taking away
distortion from D2.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:42.880
And so D2 gets smaller as
the right term gets larger.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:46.210
Yeah.

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:47.890
So that's basically the answer.

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:50.470
So I'm going to just repeat the
answer that the student gave

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:54.040
is that, oh, if my D1 is
greater than sigma 1 square,

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:58.090
then anyways my, this rate
term is going to be 0.

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:02.440
Any more component of distortion
which I give to the source 1,

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:05.350
it's basically taking
that distortion away

00:29:05.350 --> 00:29:07.810
from the second one
because my total distortion

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:09.010
needs to be constant.

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:10.360
And that's wasted anyway.

00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:15.300
So it's not reducing my
objective term at all.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:19.770
And so it doesn't make sense to
give distortion one ever greater

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:21.030
than sigma 1 square.

00:29:21.030 --> 00:29:25.610
Again, I see maybe
[AUDIO OUT] write it down.

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:32.390
Remember this thing,
what it means.

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:35.480
So this was the rate term
for a single Gaussian.

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:39.440
And this was just 1/2
log sigma square by D

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:43.250
if my D was between
0 to sigma square.

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:46.460
But it was 0 if my D was
greater than sigma square.

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:48.620
So if my distortion
which I'm allowing

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:52.700
is greater than the
variance, after a point,

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:55.500
it doesn't matter if I
[AUDIO OUT] extortion,

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:59.060
I can anyways get
away with zero rate.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:02.840
If you now allow me that OK,
you can double the distortion,

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:06.710
well, I can already get zero
rate with half that distortion.

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:10.550
WHY should I ever take
away that distortion

00:30:10.550 --> 00:30:12.740
from the other component
and give it to this one?

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:14.690
So just very intuitively
we are talking

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:16.460
about this objective function.

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.235
So the answer here
is no, you shouldn't.

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:28.760
And then what is the R of--
and what mean by R of D1

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:31.260
is this term.

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:39.840
So this you can think of
it as [AUDIO OUT] number

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:42.360
of bits which I'm going to
the first components which

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:45.750
I'm giving to the opponent.

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:49.770
And so if my again D1 is
greater than sigma 1 square,

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:52.305
how many bits am I giving
to the component 1?

00:30:59.245 --> 00:30:59.745
Anyone?

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:06.890
So I hear is 0, which is
correct because if my D1 is

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:11.690
greater than sigma 1 square,
this term is always 0.

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:15.350
Again, recall our Gaussian, if
it had higher distortion, if you

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:18.380
allow me higher distortion,
then its variance,

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:21.560
then I can just get away
with giving it 0 bits.

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:24.990
So this is just 0.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:26.030
And this is the idea.

00:31:26.030 --> 00:31:28.610
So once you hit your--

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:32.410
once you give first component
the amount of distortion D1,

00:31:32.410 --> 00:31:34.910
you don't really need to give
it extra because you're anyway

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:37.197
spending 0 bits to encode it.

00:31:37.197 --> 00:31:38.405
You can't go below than that.

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:47.630
And final question
is, what will be my--

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:50.060
this rate term if
my distortion is

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:53.180
greater than sigma 1 square
plus sigma 2 square by 2?

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:00.020
So it looks very weird.

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:01.190
But think of it.

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:09.120
My D is-- look at
this objective.

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:11.880
Your total distortion
is basically

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:15.960
the distortion you're dividing
between the two components.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:17.010
Correct.

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:20.730
So if your distortion is greater
than sigma 1 square plus sigma 2

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:23.880
square by 2, what
will be the rate,

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:25.335
how many bits do
I need to spend?

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:34.920
I see at least three, four
people with faces or hand signs

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:36.300
saying 0.

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:38.100
So yeah, that's correct.

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.020
So it's obvious, no tricks.

00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:45.060
It's 0 because then in that
case for the first component,

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:47.880
I can just allow
it sigma 1 square.

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:50.640
Distortion for the second
one, I can allow it

00:32:50.640 --> 00:32:52.440
sigma 2 square distortion.

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:55.770
And then my average
distortion in this case

00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:59.370
would be sigma 1 square
plus sigma 2 square by 2.

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:02.940
But I know in this
case I'm already

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:05.220
spending 0 bits for
the first component

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.290
and 0 bits for the
second component.

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:13.340
So once my distortion is
higher than some quantity,

00:33:13.340 --> 00:33:15.980
I actually don't need to spend
any bits because I've already

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:18.320
hit the point of maximum
distortion, which

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:22.580
I can get if I was
basically just not doing

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:25.235
anything intelligent,
not sending anything.

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:31.270
So everyone with me?

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:35.620
So, again, you can solve
this equation exactly,

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:38.500
but we just talked out
about it intuitively based

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:42.010
on what we know about Gaussian
sources and how you might--

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:44.500
what are some
intuitive points here.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:51.060
But now actually, this
is what we talked about.

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:53.960
So let's say you actually
went ahead and solved it--

00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:58.440
you could have solved
it mathematically--

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:01.108
this is what the
solution looks like.

00:34:01.108 --> 00:34:02.400
And let me walk you through it.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:05.610
It looks a bit complicated,
but it's actually not

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:07.500
that complicated.

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:11.310
So first thing to realize
is if you have a RD curve,

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:13.530
we can parameterize it by theta.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:16.830
What that means is I
can define a theta,

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:20.100
and then I can have rate as a
function of theta and distortion

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:22.230
as a function of theta.

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:23.909
So theta is just
a single variable.

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:25.889
And now rate just
depends on theta.

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:28.980
D just depends on theta.

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:31.949
So given a value
of theta, I know

00:34:31.949 --> 00:34:34.710
what my R theta and D theta is.

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:36.600
This is really my
rate distortion.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:44.510
So in that case, the solution
to this whole thing it's just,

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:46.610
again, copy-paste
it from before,

00:34:46.610 --> 00:34:51.469
is your distortions i are going
to be this minimum of theta

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:53.510
comma sigma i square.

00:34:53.510 --> 00:35:01.370
So basically your D1 is going to
be minimum of theta, comma sigma

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:02.630
1 square.

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:09.410
D2 is going to be minimum of
theta, comma sigma 2 square such

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:18.370
that your average distortion
satisfies this exactly.

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:21.760
Remember in our optimization
problem, this is an inequality.

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:24.730
But this gets satisfied exactly.

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:27.070
So you can solve
this to basically get

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:28.420
the value of theta.

00:35:28.420 --> 00:35:32.590
So given equation 1,
equation 2, and equation 3,

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:35.380
you can find out what
theta solves for this.

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:39.980
And then once you
solve for this theta,

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.200
you know what your D1 and D2 is.

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:44.940
And then your rate
is just plug back.

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:49.370
So you plug back just the
value of D1 which you got

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:51.440
and D2 which you got
in this equation.

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:55.070
So again, this is
1/2 R1 plus R2.

00:35:55.070 --> 00:35:58.800
And you just plug it back.

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:03.270
And when you solve it, this
gives you your solution.

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:07.320
And if you have done
any optimization course,

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:09.270
this is just the
Lagrange multiplier.

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:11.190
This theta which
comes from there,

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:13.830
it's not that complicated.

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:17.190
It is looking a bit complicated,
but trust me it's not.

00:36:17.190 --> 00:36:20.280
But we already have intuitively
seen what this means.

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:22.980
So think about
what it says here.

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:25.410
It basically says,
find me some theta.

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.930
If that theta is
greater than sigma 1,

00:36:27.930 --> 00:36:29.880
I'll just take sigma 1 square.

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:35.560
So my distortion D1 can never
be higher than sigma 1 square.

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:37.690
This is exactly
what we worked out.

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:40.100
And similarly, my
distortion D2 should never

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:41.350
be higher than sigma 2 square.

00:36:41.350 --> 00:36:42.267
It doesn't make sense.

00:36:42.267 --> 00:36:45.010
I'm already at rate zero then.

00:36:45.010 --> 00:36:48.070
And so I need to find
what it says additionally

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:55.220
is if your distortion is less
than sigma 1 square and sigma 2

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:59.810
square, then your D1 will be
theta 1-- sorry-- just theta.

00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:02.630
Let's say your theta is less
than sigma 1 square as well

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:07.430
as sigma 2 square, then
your D1 equals 2 then your--

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:13.220
so let's say if theta is less
than sigma 1 square, sigma 2

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:17.560
square, then your D1 is
equals to D2 equals to theta.

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:23.600
This is something which
we didn't see intuitively.

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:29.300
And this is a consequence of
solving that problem exactly,

00:37:29.300 --> 00:37:31.670
the optimization problem.

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:32.990
Sorry.

00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:35.120
But the minima
parts we saw before.

00:37:35.120 --> 00:37:37.400
So this is an
additional intuition

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:40.490
which you get through
the KKT when you actually

00:37:40.490 --> 00:37:43.730
solve it, which is if
I can, if I'm never--

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:46.940
if my splitting my distortions
exactly between the two

00:37:46.940 --> 00:37:51.470
components always stays below
the lower of my variances,

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:53.987
then just split it equally.

00:37:53.987 --> 00:37:55.570
At that point, I'm
not doing anything.

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:56.590
I could have done anything.

00:37:56.590 --> 00:37:58.540
I could have split it
proportional to variances.

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:00.957
I could have split it inversely
proportional to variances.

00:38:00.957 --> 00:38:03.750
I could have done
1,000 different things.

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:05.970
But you can show mathematically
the optimal solution

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:09.330
to this equation is
if my D is less than--

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:13.290
if my distortion,
which I can go--

00:38:13.290 --> 00:38:15.810
if I can actually split
my distortion equally

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:20.780
between the two components,
let's just do that.

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:22.820
And then this whole
minima, et cetera,

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:27.830
is just a fancy
mathematical way which

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:31.940
comes about from this exact
intuition, which we already saw.

00:38:31.940 --> 00:38:36.085
That if that component
gets a distortion higher

00:38:36.085 --> 00:38:37.460
than its variance,
don't give it.

00:38:37.460 --> 00:38:38.502
Give it to the other one.

00:38:38.502 --> 00:38:40.100
You don't really need it.

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:42.815
This is just the
mathematical way.

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:51.770
So this is exactly the
water-filling intuition.

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:56.030
Let's just-- this is the
solution to that KKT actually

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:59.090
comes up in that particular
optimization problem.

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:01.520
It actually comes up in
various different places.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:04.040
And people have come up
with this very intuitive way

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:06.620
of thinking about this,
which is really helpful.

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:09.140
And it's called this
water-filling intuition.

00:39:09.140 --> 00:39:11.660
And so let's work it out
together, what I mean

00:39:11.660 --> 00:39:13.580
by water-filling intuition.

00:39:13.580 --> 00:39:15.410
So first of all you
can agree with me,

00:39:15.410 --> 00:39:18.170
there are three cases
I need to work with.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:20.930
First one is when my D
is always less than sigma

00:39:20.930 --> 00:39:23.000
1 square, sigma 2 square.

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:25.640
In that case, I
can equally split.

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:27.800
The third one is
when my D is always

00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:30.440
greater than sigma 1 square
plus sigma 2 square by 2.

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:32.780
In that case, the
rate is going to be 0.

00:39:32.780 --> 00:39:37.100
I can always just split sigma
1 to the first component.

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:38.810
Sigma 1 square to
the first component.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:41.150
Sigma 2 square to
the second component.

00:39:41.150 --> 00:39:43.130
And then this is
the interesting one,

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:46.970
which makes the previous formula
looks more complicated than it

00:39:46.970 --> 00:39:48.410
really is.

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:52.700
So let's just see
what's happening.

00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:58.110
So case 1, let's
assume-- let me represent

00:39:58.110 --> 00:40:02.690
my two independent components
as something like this.

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:03.840
This is my component 1.

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.850
This is my component 2.

00:40:06.850 --> 00:40:09.940
And what I'm showing
on y-axis is sigma.

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:11.350
So this is sigma 1 square.

00:40:11.350 --> 00:40:14.210
This is sigma 2 square.

00:40:14.210 --> 00:40:16.700
And again, we assume sigma
1 is less than sigma 2.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:17.450
It doesn't matter.

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:23.790
So let's see.

00:40:23.790 --> 00:40:32.400
Case 1, when my D is less
than sigma 1 square and hence

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:34.530
D is less than sigma 2 square.

00:40:34.530 --> 00:40:35.070
We assumed.

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:46.770
I'm sorry.

00:40:53.220 --> 00:40:54.060
Let's see.

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:54.990
Yeah.

00:40:54.990 --> 00:40:56.100
The iPad is helpful.

00:40:56.100 --> 00:40:59.300
I don't have to redraw.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:03.530
So if your D actually
less than this,

00:41:03.530 --> 00:41:17.700
then I can split my D1 equals
to D2 to just be 1/2 of this

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:21.920
because my D by 2--

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:22.430
sorry.

00:41:22.430 --> 00:41:24.620
This should be
exactly D. My bad.

00:41:30.150 --> 00:41:30.650
Correct.

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:34.190
Because my D is less than sigma
1 square and sigma 2 square,

00:41:34.190 --> 00:41:39.240
I can just assign
my D1 to be whatever

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:41.760
distortion allowed D2 to be
whatever distortion allowed.

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:45.120
And then D1 plus
D2 by 2 is D, which

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:47.310
is less than or
equal to D. So we

00:41:47.310 --> 00:41:50.070
are within whatever
distortion bound.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:52.980
And then rate in this case--

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:53.940
let me write with this.

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:56.520
Rate in this case is
just going to be 1/2 log.

00:41:59.090 --> 00:42:00.470
Sorry.

00:42:00.470 --> 00:42:03.140
Let's write it like this in
case it's still not clear.

00:42:03.140 --> 00:42:08.060
1/2 of rate 1 and rate 2, like
some of them, and then my rate 1

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:12.170
is just 1/2 log
sigma 1 square by D

00:42:12.170 --> 00:42:16.030
plus 1/2 log sigma
2 square by D.

00:42:16.030 --> 00:42:18.190
So now you see there
is no D1 and D2

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:19.683
terms in our final expression.

00:42:19.683 --> 00:42:21.850
It's just D because that's
what you want to tell me.

00:42:21.850 --> 00:42:25.435
You told me that just give
D distortion to each one.

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:37.120
Now let's say-- now
let's look at the case 2.

00:42:37.120 --> 00:42:37.620
Sorry.

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:38.703
Let me add one more thing.

00:42:38.703 --> 00:42:40.440
So one way to
think about this is

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:44.850
assume you had one as a sigma
1 square size cup and two

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:48.330
also as a sigma 2
square size cup.

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:50.700
And so what did you do?

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:53.370
Assume you poured
water into this

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.890
and there wasn't
any barrier between,

00:42:55.890 --> 00:42:58.830
then the water would have been
basically ISO level filled

00:42:58.830 --> 00:43:00.240
in both these things.

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:03.885
So it's like you're pouring
water equally filled in both.

00:43:06.510 --> 00:43:14.320
Now let's look at case 2
when your D is in between,

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:28.800
let's say sigma 1 square and
sigma 2 square by D. Nice works.

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:33.900
So now in this case,
we saw that I'll never

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:36.990
give D1 more than this.

00:43:36.990 --> 00:43:41.580
So in this case, my D1 will
be equal to sigma 1 square

00:43:41.580 --> 00:43:44.670
because again if I
were to equally divide,

00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:46.500
my D would have
been somewhere here.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:48.030
It's greater than
sigma 1 square.

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.430
But I'm not going to
waste this much distortion

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:52.440
on the first one.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:55.980
Rather I'm going to come here
and give it to the second one.

00:43:55.980 --> 00:43:59.970
So my second one would be
2D minus sigma 1 square.

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:04.500
So this is just to ensure
that D1 plus D2 by 2 is D.

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:08.490
So this plus sigma 1
square by 2 is just

00:44:08.490 --> 00:44:14.920
D. And so this is
higher than actuality D.

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:17.530
So this is what you would
have given if it was equal,

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:20.500
but you are now giving
the second source

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:23.510
some extra distortion.

00:44:23.510 --> 00:44:25.460
Again, looking at
that cup analogy,

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:30.530
it's like if I'm filling cups
and this was at equal level,

00:44:30.530 --> 00:44:34.010
my water would overflow
the first one if it

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:35.570
was more than sigma 1 square.

00:44:35.570 --> 00:44:38.990
That overflown water goes
to the second bucket.

00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:43.490
So you get this extra water
here in the second bucket.

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:45.875
Again, this is just
D, this level here.

00:44:51.590 --> 00:45:01.010
And your RD in this case
again would be 1/2 R1 plus R2.

00:45:01.010 --> 00:45:04.820
But I know my R1 is 0
because sigma square by D,

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:07.610
I don't actually need
to give it any bits.

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:12.110
And my R2 now is
log sigma square

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:15.920
by the distortion, which is
given to the second one, which

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:19.820
is 2D minus sigma 1 square.

00:45:19.820 --> 00:45:21.420
This is really my rate.

00:45:27.670 --> 00:45:31.600
So basically what did we do?

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:34.480
We gave lower distortion to the
source which had lower variance.

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:36.370
Again, go back to
our thumb rule.

00:45:36.370 --> 00:45:39.040
So you give lower
distortion to the source

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:40.270
which had lower variance.

00:45:40.270 --> 00:45:42.400
And you actually gave
a higher distortion,

00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:44.020
in this particular
case, to the source

00:45:44.020 --> 00:45:46.240
which had higher variance.

00:45:46.240 --> 00:45:48.130
You didn't really do
it in the first case,

00:45:48.130 --> 00:45:50.380
and that's really what
the formula is saying.

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:52.180
Even though they had
different variance,

00:45:52.180 --> 00:45:54.880
you first try to
give them equally.

00:45:54.880 --> 00:45:57.610
Then you try to see,
oh, do I actually

00:45:57.610 --> 00:46:00.460
need to give that much
distortion to each source?

00:46:00.460 --> 00:46:03.580
No, I could have actually,
maybe in this particular case,

00:46:03.580 --> 00:46:06.010
gotten away by giving less.

00:46:06.010 --> 00:46:08.470
And then you basically
allow that distortion

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:11.050
to be given to the
higher variance source.

00:46:11.050 --> 00:46:12.220
Yeah.

00:46:12.220 --> 00:46:17.020
Actually, it's a
sigma 2 on the minus.

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:18.080
Where?

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:18.788
On the minus.

00:46:18.788 --> 00:46:20.080
Oh, yeah, it should be sigma 2.

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:22.790
Yes, thank you.

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:23.750
Thank you.

00:46:23.750 --> 00:46:25.280
Did I write it correct here?

00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:26.510
Yeah.

00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:28.820
Thank you.

00:46:28.820 --> 00:46:32.750
And then the final case is
now I would say very obvious.

00:46:32.750 --> 00:46:44.110
So the final case is just when
your D is greater than sigma 1,

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:45.640
so actually--

00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:46.870
sorry.

00:46:46.870 --> 00:46:50.140
Sigma 1 square plus
sigma 2 square by 2

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:51.550
actually greater than equals to.

00:46:51.550 --> 00:46:52.770
Yeah.

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:54.030
Everywhere you can take.

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:07.070
And in this particular
case again now you know.

00:47:07.070 --> 00:47:10.580
So it's exactly
like water-filling,

00:47:10.580 --> 00:47:17.460
and you would give exactly
sigma 1 square bits here,

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:19.050
sigma 2 square here.

00:47:19.050 --> 00:47:21.120
Whatever extra
distortion you have left,

00:47:21.120 --> 00:47:23.460
you actually don't even need it.

00:47:23.460 --> 00:47:25.560
And your RD is just 0.

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:31.730
Again, it's like if
you were filling water,

00:47:31.730 --> 00:47:35.540
you fill the first one till the
levels are equal, then-- sorry.

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:38.000
You start filling same levels.

00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:39.470
You are filling
both these things.

00:47:39.470 --> 00:47:41.330
At some point, you
fill the first one

00:47:41.330 --> 00:47:44.938
and whatever extra is coming
will go to the second guy.

00:47:44.938 --> 00:47:47.480
After some point, you're also
going to fill up the second guy

00:47:47.480 --> 00:47:49.220
and you don't really need it.

00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:55.970
So notice this is not
maximum of sigma 1, sigma 2.

00:47:55.970 --> 00:47:57.410
This can be lower than that.

00:47:57.410 --> 00:48:01.670
In fact APGP you can show
that it will be not even APGP.

00:48:01.670 --> 00:48:04.940
You can show that it will be
lower than maximum of sigma 1

00:48:04.940 --> 00:48:07.630
and sigma 2.

00:48:07.630 --> 00:48:13.260
And this constant you
see here in case 1

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:15.270
was the theta in our equation.

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:18.030
We saw that theta.

00:48:18.030 --> 00:48:22.080
When we solve this KKT,
we saw that if my D is

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:27.410
less than-- if I can do that,
I'll always split equally.

00:48:27.410 --> 00:48:30.110
And so this is the
water-filling intuition.

00:48:30.110 --> 00:48:33.500
Is it clear to everyone
how you are splitting?

00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:36.380
The case 2 should be
sigma 1 square less than D

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:41.770
less than sigma 1 square plus
sigma 2 square by 2 instead of--

00:48:41.770 --> 00:48:42.910
Yes, that is correct.

00:48:42.910 --> 00:48:43.510
Thank you.

00:48:43.510 --> 00:48:45.347
Yeah, this should be this guy.

00:48:45.347 --> 00:48:46.430
That's what you're saying.

00:48:46.430 --> 00:48:48.370
Yeah.

00:48:48.370 --> 00:48:49.930
We'll fix the notes too.

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:54.790
I think this might be wrong in
the notes also, so I'll fix it.

00:48:54.790 --> 00:49:01.070
So what student pointed out
was this should be sigma

00:49:01.070 --> 00:49:03.440
1 square plus sigma
2 square by 2.

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:05.060
Same thing which I
was saying I should

00:49:05.060 --> 00:49:07.880
have reflected in the notes, but
you don't really actually need

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:09.380
to go to sigma 2 even.

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:12.770
Before that, you can
just give zero rate.

00:49:12.770 --> 00:49:14.375
And we'll correct it here also.

00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:19.910
Thank you.

00:49:19.910 --> 00:49:20.600
Good catch.

00:49:24.030 --> 00:49:27.792
But intuitively, everyone
is with me, what we did.

00:49:27.792 --> 00:49:29.250
Again, this
water-filling intuition

00:49:29.250 --> 00:49:30.885
is an optimization case.

00:49:30.885 --> 00:49:32.010
It's a fancy way of saying.

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.720
But now you understand
what's happening,

00:49:33.720 --> 00:49:36.270
how are you
distributing your bits

00:49:36.270 --> 00:49:40.020
for two independent Gaussian
sources when you were actually

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:42.330
encoding them in
blocks of size 2.

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:49.200
And so this is again instead of
measure, this should be level.

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:54.540
So again remember our thumb rule
which is for a given distortion

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:56.790
level, we can allocate
more bits to components

00:49:56.790 --> 00:49:59.040
with higher variance
because we can

00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:03.000
allow higher distortion for
components with higher variance.

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:06.900
And for block of two components,
we did the same thing.

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:09.690
So given, obviously,
there is this only case 2

00:50:09.690 --> 00:50:12.030
where this comes about.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:13.890
But more generally,
you can think of it

00:50:13.890 --> 00:50:18.240
as that we can allocate
more bits to components

00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:21.090
with higher variance.

00:50:21.090 --> 00:50:23.080
And this shows up in
various different ways.

00:50:32.260 --> 00:50:35.020
Any questions because
now we're going

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:38.020
to use this to motivate
the next topic, which is I

00:50:38.020 --> 00:50:40.120
would say very
central and integral

00:50:40.120 --> 00:50:43.180
to a lot of lossy
compressors out there,

00:50:43.180 --> 00:50:45.790
basically everything, every
lossy compressor out there.

00:50:51.285 --> 00:50:52.410
I guess everyone's with me.

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:53.820
So that's great.

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.770
So just a few
comments, so we looked

00:50:55.770 --> 00:51:01.230
into a very specific example of
uncorrelated Gaussian sources.

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:02.430
Sorry.

00:51:02.430 --> 00:51:05.040
Uncorrelated Gaussian sources.

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:07.042
And these are just
the takeaways.

00:51:07.042 --> 00:51:09.000
We saw that you can use
water-filling intuition

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:11.910
to basically selectively
allocate bits

00:51:11.910 --> 00:51:14.490
to different components.

00:51:14.490 --> 00:51:17.430
And I'm not going
to cover this but--

00:51:17.430 --> 00:51:20.965
so again we talked about
stochastic processes

00:51:20.965 --> 00:51:21.840
when we were talking.

00:51:21.840 --> 00:51:26.310
So here we just use the block
part of the Shannon's RD theorem

00:51:26.310 --> 00:51:28.260
because where did
we use it actually,

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:30.270
if you have to ask
where did we use it?

00:51:30.270 --> 00:51:33.480
We actually used it when we--

00:51:33.480 --> 00:51:37.380
where is it when-- we did
the step, when we wrote it

00:51:37.380 --> 00:51:39.960
like this because this
1/2 log sigma square by D

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:42.120
actually comes from
the mutual information.

00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:43.920
So that's where we
use the Shannon's RD

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:46.050
theorem for Gaussian case.

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:47.880
But actually what
you can show is

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:51.330
it also generalizes very
beautifully to this correlated

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:54.360
Gaussian processes because
Gaussian processes have

00:51:54.360 --> 00:51:56.070
lots of very nice properties.

00:51:56.070 --> 00:51:59.730
You can use them to decorrelate.

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:03.810
Basically whatever we did, you
add couple more steps to it.

00:52:03.810 --> 00:52:05.430
And you can show
that you can do it

00:52:05.430 --> 00:52:07.860
for actually a very high
dimensional correlated

00:52:07.860 --> 00:52:08.700
Gaussian sources.

00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:11.550
I'm not going to cover this
here, but look at the notes

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:14.610
that has some
intuition or if you

00:52:14.610 --> 00:52:16.920
have done signal processing
previously and comfortable

00:52:16.920 --> 00:52:19.290
with these topics, we
can also talk about it.

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:26.940
But in life in general, we
don't have Gaussian sources.

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:30.750
We don't have-- we'll
actually have correlated

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:33.870
non-Gaussian sources.

00:52:33.870 --> 00:52:36.270
And we'll probably
need to do something

00:52:36.270 --> 00:52:39.600
more sophisticated than
just this water-filling.

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:41.460
And that's the idea
of transform coding.

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:44.370
So transform coding,
this is how I would

00:52:44.370 --> 00:52:45.720
think about transform coding.

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:47.950
There are various different
ways to think about it.

00:52:47.950 --> 00:52:50.400
But the basic idea is
you transform the source

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:53.670
to a different
domain, which allows

00:52:53.670 --> 00:52:55.680
you to have
decorrelated components

00:52:55.680 --> 00:52:57.210
with different variances.

00:52:57.210 --> 00:53:01.020
So this is not what transform
coding is, all the definition.

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:03.840
I'm just trying to motivate
what we are trying to do.

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:06.180
So we are transforming
our source

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:08.550
to some other
domain where we can

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:13.650
use the intuition or the thumb
rules we got from this Gaussian

00:53:13.650 --> 00:53:17.430
source, which is it allows
you to first decorrelate

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:19.650
the components so that
makes your life easier.

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:22.420
Not only that, it allows you
to decorrelate components

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:24.970
with different
variances or a term

00:53:24.970 --> 00:53:29.080
called energy, which
we'll see in a bit.

00:53:29.080 --> 00:53:32.500
And so since it allows you to
get this decorrelated component

00:53:32.500 --> 00:53:35.110
with different
variances, now you can--

00:53:35.110 --> 00:53:38.440
it's not Gaussian, sure,
but again thumb rule.

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:41.560
You can think of doing the exact
same thing which we did with--

00:53:41.560 --> 00:53:45.430
which we worked out exactly for
Gaussian uncorrelated sources.

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:47.740
So in the transform
domain then, we

00:53:47.740 --> 00:53:49.810
can use this
water-filling intuition

00:53:49.810 --> 00:53:53.980
to now selectively allocate
bits to different components.

00:53:53.980 --> 00:53:56.922
So you can be smart about
how do I allocate bits

00:53:56.922 --> 00:53:58.630
to the first component,
second component,

00:53:58.630 --> 00:54:02.470
and that's how you can improve
the compression performance.

00:54:07.530 --> 00:54:10.950
So this is just a repeat
of the earlier slide.

00:54:10.950 --> 00:54:12.870
So we had some
symbols, K symbols

00:54:12.870 --> 00:54:15.990
encode codebook bits
decode reconstruction.

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:18.330
Now I'm going very fast
for you guys should

00:54:18.330 --> 00:54:20.640
be able to do the same.

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:23.220
What we do in transform
coding is basically,

00:54:23.220 --> 00:54:26.700
let's first look at the diagram,
you insert a block in the middle

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:29.420
before encoder.

00:54:29.420 --> 00:54:32.480
So this is some X bar.

00:54:32.480 --> 00:54:35.390
So this is same as Xk tuple.

00:54:35.390 --> 00:54:37.070
In the notes somewhere
we use X bar,

00:54:37.070 --> 00:54:41.870
so I decided to continue with
that so that it's not confusing.

00:54:41.870 --> 00:54:46.130
So these are the same
things, Xk, X bar for now.

00:54:46.130 --> 00:54:50.550
So you have some, let's
say, k dimensional vector.

00:54:50.550 --> 00:54:56.250
You first transform this
to some other domain,

00:54:56.250 --> 00:54:59.760
which is just applying this
function to this vector.

00:54:59.760 --> 00:55:05.290
And now you encode
this Y, and we

00:55:05.290 --> 00:55:07.630
go through same other pipeline.

00:55:07.630 --> 00:55:11.740
So all you have added
is this transform step.

00:55:11.740 --> 00:55:15.640
And similarly in
the decode side,

00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:18.190
we have this transform inverse.

00:55:18.190 --> 00:55:22.390
So you get Y. Now you do
all the same things which

00:55:22.390 --> 00:55:24.820
we have been doing so far,
scalar quantization, vector

00:55:24.820 --> 00:55:27.290
quantization, RD,
whatever you want to do.

00:55:27.290 --> 00:55:29.020
You encode it into
bits, decode it.

00:55:29.020 --> 00:55:30.430
You get back Y hat.

00:55:30.430 --> 00:55:32.500
And then you can apply
the inverse transform

00:55:32.500 --> 00:55:36.310
to recover your X hat.

00:55:38.850 --> 00:55:42.300
For this class, let's
assume your T is linear.

00:55:42.300 --> 00:55:43.980
And I just told
you why you would

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:49.230
want to do that because the hope
is even though X has correlated

00:55:49.230 --> 00:55:52.530
components, Y basically you
get uncorrelated components

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:54.600
with various different
variances, which allows

00:55:54.600 --> 00:55:56.550
you to efficiently encode it.

00:55:56.550 --> 00:55:59.280
For this class, you can
assume that T is linear.

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:01.770
But for those of you who
are familiar with machine

00:56:01.770 --> 00:56:05.130
learning these days, your
T need not be linear.

00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:07.260
It can be something
extremely complicated.

00:56:07.260 --> 00:56:09.960
Pick your favorite large
neural net and data,

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:13.470
it can be some very fancy
non-linear transform.

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:16.020
And we'll see in learned
image compression.

00:56:16.020 --> 00:56:18.540
We're going to build slowly
how we are going to use this.

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:20.100
And during learned
image compression,

00:56:20.100 --> 00:56:23.820
we'll see one example of how you
can have some learned transform.

00:56:27.510 --> 00:56:33.015
So one way to think about this
is your X bar is a vector.

00:56:36.220 --> 00:56:42.820
Your T is a matrix,
which gives you

00:56:42.820 --> 00:56:45.700
Y bar, which is again a vector.

00:56:45.700 --> 00:56:51.060
And then so if this
X was k dimensional,

00:56:51.060 --> 00:56:53.790
we'll get Y which
is k dimensional.

00:56:53.790 --> 00:56:55.740
Again, the important
part really is your T

00:56:55.740 --> 00:56:59.250
should be invertible so that
you can actually recover back

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:02.190
X from Y. That's
really important.

00:57:02.190 --> 00:57:05.460
This linear thing,
this whole linear thing

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:08.260
is an assumption which
we'll work with today.

00:57:13.860 --> 00:57:18.180
So again just to repeat once
more if it wasn't clear,

00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:19.650
why transform coding?

00:57:19.650 --> 00:57:23.010
The idea should be--
that's really important.

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:25.200
So first is decorrelation.

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:26.940
So X can be correlated.

00:57:26.940 --> 00:57:30.180
But once you do all this
stuff, multiplied with matrix,

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:33.810
your Y can be decorrelated.

00:57:33.810 --> 00:57:35.790
And decorrelation
basically allows you

00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:37.320
for efficient quantization.

00:57:37.320 --> 00:57:40.410
So it might have taken you
some large vector quantizer

00:57:40.410 --> 00:57:46.030
to actually get similar
compression level [AUDIO OUT]

00:57:46.030 --> 00:57:49.020
simple quantizer like
scalar [AUDIO OUT]

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:52.090
the complexity of things.

00:57:52.090 --> 00:57:55.470
And then another idea
is energy compaction.

00:57:55.470 --> 00:57:58.350
And so this is another way
of saying that variance idea

00:57:58.350 --> 00:57:59.220
really.

00:57:59.220 --> 00:58:03.690
But the idea is you would
ideally want maybe more energy.

00:58:03.690 --> 00:58:05.760
So we'll define
energy in a second.

00:58:05.760 --> 00:58:09.360
But basically you want more
information in some sense,

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:12.660
in the first component
than in the second one.

00:58:12.660 --> 00:58:15.030
So you can think of
it as, oh, if I'm

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:16.860
breaking my thing
into two components

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:19.140
similar to that
Gaussian case, I want

00:58:19.140 --> 00:58:22.380
one component to be something
which I really need to preserve.

00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:25.010
The other one maybe
I can throw away.

00:58:25.010 --> 00:58:27.080
And then what you can
do if you can break it

00:58:27.080 --> 00:58:28.640
into two components
like that, you

00:58:28.640 --> 00:58:30.410
can allocate more
bits and distortion

00:58:30.410 --> 00:58:33.950
to the more important component
and less bits and distortion

00:58:33.950 --> 00:58:36.940
to the less important component.

00:58:36.940 --> 00:58:42.310
And that's really the way you
steal away performance or bits.

00:58:42.310 --> 00:58:46.090
So transform allows for both of
these things, so decorrelation

00:58:46.090 --> 00:58:48.040
as well as energy compaction.

00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:49.750
So energy compaction
really is what's

00:58:49.750 --> 00:58:55.960
allowing you to allocate bits to
basically different components

00:58:55.960 --> 00:59:00.090
of your block in a
more efficient manner.

00:59:00.090 --> 00:59:02.790
So if I have to translate
this to a Gaussian case,

00:59:02.790 --> 00:59:05.550
it's like the source which
had a higher variance

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:08.248
is more important source for me
because it's really hard for me

00:59:08.248 --> 00:59:08.790
to encode it.

00:59:08.790 --> 00:59:10.082
It can have very high variance.

00:59:10.082 --> 00:59:11.160
It can jump over.

00:59:11.160 --> 00:59:14.100
So I'll spend more bits
in just understanding.

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:16.080
That and then I'll
spend less bits

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:18.240
if the other one is
just always a constant.

00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:21.090
I don't have to worry or very
low sigma I don't have to worry

00:59:21.090 --> 00:59:22.470
about spending bits there.

00:59:27.110 --> 00:59:29.030
So these two are--

00:59:29.030 --> 00:59:32.960
so again, T is something
which we are designing.

00:59:32.960 --> 00:59:33.875
Nobody is giving you.

00:59:33.875 --> 00:59:36.920
You can come up with any T in
the world in life you want,

00:59:36.920 --> 00:59:38.720
but obviously it should
serve some purpose

00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:39.920
to help us with the lossy.

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:42.650
So one property we
saw was, OK, it needs

00:59:42.650 --> 00:59:44.660
to be somewhat invertible.

00:59:44.660 --> 00:59:46.550
Actually this is also
not hard property

00:59:46.550 --> 00:59:49.970
because in non-linear case,
these are not exact inverses.

00:59:49.970 --> 00:59:51.290
But it works out fine.

00:59:51.290 --> 00:59:53.780
We'll see that idea
little bit more.

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:57.410
But then again, these are, I
would say, not requirements.

00:59:57.410 --> 00:59:59.330
These are more like,
oh, it would be

00:59:59.330 --> 01:00:01.700
nice to have these properties.

01:00:01.700 --> 01:00:04.160
So if it decorrelates
more, great.

01:00:04.160 --> 01:00:07.310
It helps me, makes my
further steps simpler.

01:00:07.310 --> 01:00:09.860
If I can get energy
compaction, again, it then

01:00:09.860 --> 01:00:14.090
helps me selectively
distribute bits better.

01:00:14.090 --> 01:00:16.280
And so since I am
allowed to choose to T,

01:00:16.280 --> 01:00:18.500
my first assumption
was, OK, T is linear.

01:00:18.500 --> 01:00:22.190
Actually, let me make a even
stronger assumption that T

01:00:22.190 --> 01:00:25.730
is an orthonormal matrix.

01:00:25.730 --> 01:00:29.480
So as soon as I
said orthonormal,

01:00:29.480 --> 01:00:30.890
I saw some confusing faces.

01:00:30.890 --> 01:00:35.390
So I'm happy I did this
quick review slide.

01:00:35.390 --> 01:00:37.850
But, yeah, so we are going to
use a bit of linear algebra

01:00:37.850 --> 01:00:39.350
and signal processing
in this class,

01:00:39.350 --> 01:00:40.642
again, like we have been doing.

01:00:40.642 --> 01:00:42.830
It's not-- we'll
give you the basics.

01:00:42.830 --> 01:00:45.890
So let's just quickly go over--

01:00:45.890 --> 01:00:48.620
again, this is just-- you
can take any linear algebra

01:00:48.620 --> 01:00:53.600
book or notes and this
is very simple concepts.

01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:54.680
We'll go over this.

01:00:54.680 --> 01:00:57.560
But after this class if
you are not able to follow,

01:00:57.560 --> 01:00:58.227
come talk to us.

01:00:58.227 --> 01:00:59.768
If you don't have
background in this,

01:00:59.768 --> 01:01:01.760
we can give material
or explain this better.

01:01:01.760 --> 01:01:05.120
So I'll be going a little fast
assuming people have seen it

01:01:05.120 --> 01:01:08.847
and only need revision, not
really defining and explaining

01:01:08.847 --> 01:01:09.680
things from scratch.

01:01:12.830 --> 01:01:17.330
So what are
orthonormal matrices?

01:01:17.330 --> 01:01:22.340
So consider your y equals to AX,
is just a matrix vector product.

01:01:22.340 --> 01:01:24.830
Then we call A
orthonormal, which

01:01:24.830 --> 01:01:29.360
we'll denote as U if U
transpose U equals to identity.

01:01:29.360 --> 01:01:31.310
So U it's a square matrix.

01:01:31.310 --> 01:01:33.110
Maybe I should
have written that.

01:01:33.110 --> 01:01:35.300
So is A square matrix.

01:01:39.090 --> 01:01:43.980
Actually, let's write
it U is a square matrix.

01:01:43.980 --> 01:01:46.050
Everyone with me?

01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:48.510
I can just assume this is fine.

01:01:48.510 --> 01:01:50.480
Anyone?

01:01:50.480 --> 01:01:50.980
OK.

01:01:50.980 --> 01:01:52.750
Good.

01:01:52.750 --> 01:01:56.330
So then the square of
the Euclidean norm,

01:01:56.330 --> 01:02:00.580
it's what's called energy like
what we saw, energy compaction

01:02:00.580 --> 01:02:01.450
term really.

01:02:01.450 --> 01:02:03.100
It's also called energy.

01:02:03.100 --> 01:02:07.180
And what you can show that it is
preserved under an orthonormal

01:02:07.180 --> 01:02:08.920
transform.

01:02:08.920 --> 01:02:13.050
So if you write
norm of Y square,

01:02:13.050 --> 01:02:16.230
you can write it as Y
transpose Y. This is just

01:02:16.230 --> 01:02:17.970
the definition of norm.

01:02:17.970 --> 01:02:19.770
This is X transpose.

01:02:19.770 --> 01:02:23.500
So Y is just UX.

01:02:23.500 --> 01:02:24.400
Let's write it down.

01:02:24.400 --> 01:02:26.320
Y is UX.

01:02:26.320 --> 01:02:31.510
Therefore, Y transpose is
X transpose U transpose.

01:02:31.510 --> 01:02:33.310
So this is just Y transpose.

01:02:33.310 --> 01:02:37.600
This is Y. This definition
is not doing anything fancy.

01:02:37.600 --> 01:02:40.450
U transpose U is identity.

01:02:40.450 --> 01:02:43.300
By our definition, so
this gives X transpose X,

01:02:43.300 --> 01:02:46.750
which is just norm of X square.

01:02:46.750 --> 01:02:52.060
You can also write this as y1
square plus y2 square plus dot

01:02:52.060 --> 01:02:54.490
dot dot whatever, yk square.

01:02:57.830 --> 01:03:01.100
And for those of
you who are maybe

01:03:01.100 --> 01:03:03.290
familiar with signal
processing and one

01:03:03.290 --> 01:03:07.220
specific orthonormal transform
which is this Fourier transform,

01:03:07.220 --> 01:03:09.890
you might have heard
of Parseval's theorem.

01:03:09.890 --> 01:03:13.160
This is just a specific case
of this particular thing.

01:03:13.160 --> 01:03:15.660
It's just generally true
for any orthonormal matrix.

01:03:20.880 --> 01:03:25.380
What it really means is that
the energy in transform domain

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:27.330
matches the energy
in the original.

01:03:29.960 --> 01:03:34.180
And this is really
reading it out in English.

01:03:34.180 --> 01:03:37.240
Similarly, what you can show
that is this transform preserves

01:03:37.240 --> 01:03:40.460
Euclidean distance
between points.

01:03:40.460 --> 01:03:44.750
What that means is assume
your Y1 is UX1, Y2 is UX2.

01:03:44.750 --> 01:03:49.640
And if I'm calculating the L2
distance square, in this case,

01:03:49.640 --> 01:03:52.310
Y1 minus Y2 norm
whole square, you

01:03:52.310 --> 01:03:56.580
can show that this is equal
to X1 minus X2 whole square.

01:03:56.580 --> 01:03:58.140
Again, it's the same thing.

01:03:58.140 --> 01:04:01.710
Instead of Y here, I
just put Y1 minus Y2

01:04:01.710 --> 01:04:04.890
and you'll get a U transpose
U, which becomes identity

01:04:04.890 --> 01:04:07.410
and that will give you
this X1 minus X2 square.

01:04:07.410 --> 01:04:08.790
So just work it out.

01:04:08.790 --> 01:04:11.400
It's just directly
the definition.

01:04:11.400 --> 01:04:13.560
And this is very cool.

01:04:13.560 --> 01:04:16.500
So this what it's
really saying is

01:04:16.500 --> 01:04:19.230
if I have a mean square
error distortion,

01:04:19.230 --> 01:04:22.230
then my expected value of
our mean squared distortion

01:04:22.230 --> 01:04:26.220
for symbols X is going
to be the same for Y.

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:29.950
But remember Y is now
in my transform domain.

01:04:29.950 --> 01:04:32.935
And this is why
MSE is also very--

01:04:32.935 --> 01:04:35.380
you will see a lot of
theoretical work et cetera

01:04:35.380 --> 01:04:37.180
on MSE being used.

01:04:37.180 --> 01:04:41.320
It's because even in this
orthogonal transform sense,

01:04:41.320 --> 01:04:44.890
you are basically able to
predict the same distortion.

01:04:44.890 --> 01:04:47.287
Whatever distortion you get
in the transform domain,

01:04:47.287 --> 01:04:49.120
you can say you will
get the same distortion

01:04:49.120 --> 01:04:52.077
for the original symbols.

01:04:52.077 --> 01:04:53.160
So that's a nice property.

01:04:53.160 --> 01:04:55.950
That means I can just
treat my transform domain

01:04:55.950 --> 01:04:57.870
symbols as if I was
compressing these things,

01:04:57.870 --> 01:04:59.520
do the best for
them, and I know it

01:04:59.520 --> 01:05:03.090
will translate as is to
the original source domain.

01:05:07.820 --> 01:05:10.350
Any questions?

01:05:10.350 --> 01:05:13.530
So another thing which will
be useful for the remainder

01:05:13.530 --> 01:05:16.980
of the lecture is this idea
of eigenvalue decomposition

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:18.300
or decorrelation.

01:05:18.300 --> 01:05:22.028
Again, this is a quick
review but if you guys--

01:05:22.028 --> 01:05:23.070
this is just to brush up.

01:05:23.070 --> 01:05:25.620
Again, we can give you
notes or come talk to us.

01:05:25.620 --> 01:05:28.560
But I'm assuming you have
seen this sometime in life.

01:05:28.560 --> 01:05:31.290
So this is not me presenting
this assuming you have never

01:05:31.290 --> 01:05:32.130
seen this in life.

01:05:32.130 --> 01:05:33.630
If you have never
seen this in life,

01:05:33.630 --> 01:05:36.370
definitely come talk to
one of the teaching staff.

01:05:36.370 --> 01:05:38.730
We'll send you some
material but yeah.

01:05:38.730 --> 01:05:42.240
So suppose you have
a symmetric matrix A,

01:05:42.240 --> 01:05:44.550
you can actually
decompose that matrix

01:05:44.550 --> 01:05:50.400
into product of three different
matrices, U, this capital

01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:55.440
lambda, U transpose, where
U is an orthonormal matrix.

01:05:55.440 --> 01:05:58.830
And this lambda is
a diagonal matrix.

01:05:58.830 --> 01:06:06.620
So what this lambda looks
like is some diagonal matrix.

01:06:06.620 --> 01:06:07.820
And then this is all zero.

01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:10.130
This is all zero.

01:06:10.130 --> 01:06:12.560
And what you can show
is you can compute

01:06:12.560 --> 01:06:14.900
this decomposition
for any matrix A.

01:06:14.900 --> 01:06:18.860
And when you do that, you can
show that your U is basically

01:06:18.860 --> 01:06:23.990
a matrix of normalized
eigenvectors of A. So for those

01:06:23.990 --> 01:06:27.770
of you who have forgotten,
just as a reminder,

01:06:27.770 --> 01:06:31.860
your eigenvectors
are vectors which

01:06:31.860 --> 01:06:36.540
when you apply matrix to that
vector, you apply that product.

01:06:36.540 --> 01:06:38.700
You make the matrix
vector product.

01:06:38.700 --> 01:06:40.470
You get something which scales.

01:06:40.470 --> 01:06:47.360
So lambda is a scalar, which
just scales your vector U1.

01:06:47.360 --> 01:06:50.390
And so what you can
show your matrix U,

01:06:50.390 --> 01:06:55.570
I'll just write it here, your
matrix U will turn out to be U1,

01:06:55.570 --> 01:07:05.280
U2 dot dot dot, let's
say, where U1, U2, Un

01:07:05.280 --> 01:07:08.850
are the n eigenvectors
for this matrix.

01:07:08.850 --> 01:07:11.370
And then your
matrix lambda would

01:07:11.370 --> 01:07:13.980
be the corresponding
eigenvectors

01:07:13.980 --> 01:07:15.270
in the diagonal term.

01:07:18.570 --> 01:07:21.880
Everything else zero.

01:07:21.880 --> 01:07:24.700
And you can select U such
that it's orthonormal.

01:07:24.700 --> 01:07:28.920
So you U transpose
U as identity.

01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:29.610
OK.

01:07:29.610 --> 01:07:31.920
So again if you are
seeing this first time,

01:07:31.920 --> 01:07:33.810
I know you wouldn't
really follow,

01:07:33.810 --> 01:07:36.420
but the hope is you have seen
this at some point in your life.

01:07:36.420 --> 01:07:39.900
And this is just reminding you
guys, OK, whenever we saw it,

01:07:39.900 --> 01:07:43.140
there was something like this.

01:07:43.140 --> 01:07:48.690
And so then the main idea
really is how you should--

01:07:48.690 --> 01:07:50.910
like, oh, why did
Pulkit suddenly

01:07:50.910 --> 01:07:54.270
started talking about
eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

01:07:54.270 --> 01:07:58.230
So main idea is just you see you
get doing this decomposition,

01:07:58.230 --> 01:08:00.210
you get something
which is orthonormal,

01:08:00.210 --> 01:08:01.830
an orthonormal matrix.

01:08:01.830 --> 01:08:05.230
We can treat this
as a transform.

01:08:05.230 --> 01:08:06.700
We'll talk about what it is.

01:08:06.700 --> 01:08:11.440
But some matrix say, I can
get U, which is orthonormal

01:08:11.440 --> 01:08:13.540
and then I can use
this as a transform.

01:08:16.359 --> 01:08:20.319
So let's say I use U
transpose as my transform

01:08:20.319 --> 01:08:26.290
where I define my U based on
this eigenvector decomposition.

01:08:26.290 --> 01:08:31.750
Then your Y's are U transpose
X. And what you can show

01:08:31.750 --> 01:08:38.080
is that for these Y's, these
Y's are actually decorrelated.

01:08:38.080 --> 01:08:39.939
And how you show is this.

01:08:39.939 --> 01:08:41.649
It's also there in this.

01:08:41.649 --> 01:08:43.224
Let me just do it in a new one.

01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:52.290
So let's say I have
covariance matrix for X which

01:08:52.290 --> 01:08:54.580
I'm going to call sigma.

01:08:54.580 --> 01:08:58.439
I'm hoping everybody has seen
a covariance matrix once.

01:09:01.260 --> 01:09:04.560
Let's say my X is demeaned,
so there is no mean.

01:09:04.560 --> 01:09:07.779
So let's say this is
just demean vector.

01:09:07.779 --> 01:09:12.060
So this is
expectation of X is 0.

01:09:12.060 --> 01:09:15.930
Then I can treat this
sigma as my matrix

01:09:15.930 --> 01:09:17.850
A, which was previously.

01:09:17.850 --> 01:09:21.479
And I can do an eigenvalue
decomposition, which will

01:09:21.479 --> 01:09:25.979
give me U lambda U transpose.

01:09:25.979 --> 01:09:32.350
I take this U and I get
Y, which is U transpose

01:09:32.350 --> 01:09:39.529
X. Now if I look at the
covariance matrix of Y,

01:09:39.529 --> 01:09:45.380
so that's going to be expected
value of yy transpose, which

01:09:45.380 --> 01:09:47.029
is-- we'll just substitute.

01:09:47.029 --> 01:09:52.700
This is, so my y is this.

01:09:52.700 --> 01:09:54.800
y transpose is this.

01:10:07.960 --> 01:10:08.740
Sorry.

01:10:08.740 --> 01:10:14.110
And then remember my
matrix U is fixed.

01:10:14.110 --> 01:10:15.550
This is fixed.

01:10:15.550 --> 01:10:16.780
This is not random.

01:10:20.900 --> 01:10:24.980
So I can take the expectation
terms inside, just

01:10:24.980 --> 01:10:27.200
linearity of expectations.

01:10:27.200 --> 01:10:31.790
So this is just U transpose
expected value of xx transpose

01:10:31.790 --> 01:10:37.520
U. And this is just our sigma.

01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:41.870
So this is let me call it
sigma X, just to be very clear.

01:10:44.410 --> 01:10:49.220
And so this is just U
transpose sigma X U.

01:10:49.220 --> 01:10:52.980
And make sure to have
written this here.

01:10:52.980 --> 01:11:00.720
What this implies also
is U transpose sigma X U

01:11:00.720 --> 01:11:04.970
is this diagonal matrix lambda.

01:11:04.970 --> 01:11:06.620
Yes.

01:11:06.620 --> 01:11:09.470
This is just using
U transpose U is I.

01:11:09.470 --> 01:11:14.120
And then you can left
multiply and right multiply.

01:11:14.120 --> 01:11:16.460
So what you got
here is your sigma

01:11:16.460 --> 01:11:25.300
y as a diagonal matrix, lambda
1, lambda 2, let's say lambda n.

01:11:28.210 --> 01:11:34.290
And so you see the Y's now
are completely uncorrelated.

01:11:34.290 --> 01:11:38.760
So we actually looked
into a transform.

01:11:38.760 --> 01:11:43.780
We looked into a transform
which completely decorrelates.

01:11:43.780 --> 01:11:45.850
So this is a very nice--

01:11:45.850 --> 01:11:48.430
so we already saw this
particular transform,

01:11:48.430 --> 01:11:51.580
which I'm going to call
eigenvalue decomposition

01:11:51.580 --> 01:11:53.080
or decorrelation transform.

01:11:53.080 --> 01:11:57.970
It already clearly satisfies
one of our requirements,

01:11:57.970 --> 01:12:04.120
which we started with,
which is it can decorrelate.

01:12:04.120 --> 01:12:09.400
And how it really worked is
it's basically, what is it?

01:12:09.400 --> 01:12:14.080
It's U transpose where U
was just the eigenvalues.

01:12:14.080 --> 01:12:14.580
Sorry.

01:12:14.580 --> 01:12:18.330
U is just a matrix made of
eigenvectors of my correlation

01:12:18.330 --> 01:12:19.800
matrix.

01:12:19.800 --> 01:12:22.120
So, again, this is--

01:12:22.120 --> 01:12:24.640
if you have seen
this before, you

01:12:24.640 --> 01:12:26.920
know what I did, otherwise,
just go home, read

01:12:26.920 --> 01:12:29.620
through this a little bit
and come back and talk to us.

01:12:29.620 --> 01:12:31.960
We can explain this further.

01:12:31.960 --> 01:12:34.300
In the interest of time, I'm
going to skip the example.

01:12:34.300 --> 01:12:38.510
But first, I want to give
you a punch line really.

01:12:38.510 --> 01:12:44.090
So the transform, which
we really looked at,

01:12:44.090 --> 01:12:45.350
it has a fancy--

01:12:45.350 --> 01:12:47.060
it's a named transform.

01:12:47.060 --> 01:12:50.580
It's also called
Karhunen-Loeve Transform.

01:12:50.580 --> 01:12:51.080
Sorry.

01:12:51.080 --> 01:12:54.890
French names, my
pronunciations may be wrong.

01:12:54.890 --> 01:12:57.692
It's a very famous transform
basically in signal processing.

01:12:57.692 --> 01:12:59.150
If you have done
signal processing,

01:12:59.150 --> 01:13:02.240
you might have seen
that at some point.

01:13:02.240 --> 01:13:05.870
So it's basically-- it's
exactly the steps which we did.

01:13:05.870 --> 01:13:08.300
So it's an eigenvalue-based
linear transform.

01:13:08.300 --> 01:13:11.210
So it was linear because we
were just multiplying a matrix.

01:13:11.210 --> 01:13:15.410
And its eigenvalue-based
because we

01:13:15.410 --> 01:13:17.720
computed the eigenvalues
and eigenvectors and that

01:13:17.720 --> 01:13:20.720
was the procedure.

01:13:20.720 --> 01:13:23.390
And something that I
want to say without proof

01:13:23.390 --> 01:13:27.530
is that KLT is actually optimal
transform for a given covariance

01:13:27.530 --> 01:13:29.850
matrix sigma.

01:13:29.850 --> 01:13:31.380
What I mean by optimal--

01:13:31.380 --> 01:13:33.240
so I haven't really
defined optimal,

01:13:33.240 --> 01:13:35.145
like what optimal in what sense.

01:13:37.930 --> 01:13:41.650
What we mean by optimal
really is that it maximally

01:13:41.650 --> 01:13:45.070
reduces the correlation between
the transformed components.

01:13:48.130 --> 01:13:50.950
So basically the
output is decorrelated.

01:13:50.950 --> 01:13:52.330
So we worked in a simple case.

01:13:52.330 --> 01:13:54.520
You can also have a bit
more complicated cases,

01:13:54.520 --> 01:13:58.360
but it basically decorrelate
your output transform.

01:13:58.360 --> 01:14:00.280
And not only that,
what you can show

01:14:00.280 --> 01:14:04.210
is that they are
uncorrelated and they

01:14:04.210 --> 01:14:07.030
are ordered in decreasing
order of variance.

01:14:07.030 --> 01:14:11.290
So again, if you have done--

01:14:11.290 --> 01:14:14.350
not decreasing order of variance
but basically what you can show

01:14:14.350 --> 01:14:21.220
is, let's say your lambda 1 was
greater than or equal to lambda

01:14:21.220 --> 01:14:25.030
2 dot dot lambda n.

01:14:25.030 --> 01:14:28.480
In that case, you have
basically gotten components

01:14:28.480 --> 01:14:36.330
y1 to yn such that your
variance y1 is lambda 1.

01:14:36.330 --> 01:14:39.900
Your variance yi is lambda i.

01:14:44.030 --> 01:14:45.770
So does this remind
you of something

01:14:45.770 --> 01:14:47.135
we saw earlier in the class?

01:14:51.070 --> 01:14:53.170
Sorry.

01:14:53.170 --> 01:14:53.890
Yeah.

01:14:53.890 --> 01:14:55.480
The energy compaction
idea, right?

01:14:55.480 --> 01:14:59.470
Because now my n
different components

01:14:59.470 --> 01:15:01.900
have n different variances.

01:15:01.900 --> 01:15:04.990
And they are really
what it's optimal is

01:15:04.990 --> 01:15:06.430
they are maximally spread out.

01:15:06.430 --> 01:15:07.180
You can show that.

01:15:07.180 --> 01:15:09.880
So if you know about linear
algebra, I'll just repeat it.

01:15:09.880 --> 01:15:12.250
If not, it might
go over your head.

01:15:12.250 --> 01:15:15.400
So you can think about
matrix vector factorization

01:15:15.400 --> 01:15:17.650
as just transformation
of your vector,

01:15:17.650 --> 01:15:20.350
just rotating and
changing its length.

01:15:20.350 --> 01:15:23.920
Your eigenvector
basis is basically

01:15:23.920 --> 01:15:26.110
the basis along which your--

01:15:26.110 --> 01:15:28.643
you don't change the directions.

01:15:28.643 --> 01:15:29.560
You fix the direction.

01:15:29.560 --> 01:15:31.900
And you can show that
this is the maximum value

01:15:31.900 --> 01:15:33.760
you can change your vectors by.

01:15:33.760 --> 01:15:39.260
And so basically you can show
that no matter what transform

01:15:39.260 --> 01:15:40.970
you would have
done, you wouldn't

01:15:40.970 --> 01:15:45.170
have been able to find
a component which had--

01:15:45.170 --> 01:15:46.683
I'm thinking a condition number.

01:15:46.683 --> 01:15:48.350
I'm trying to think
how to tone it down.

01:15:48.350 --> 01:15:52.310
But basically, you can show that
the ratio between the maximum

01:15:52.310 --> 01:15:55.310
and the minimum variance for
the uncorrelated components

01:15:55.310 --> 01:15:58.010
which you would have
gotten is maximally--

01:15:58.010 --> 01:15:59.870
what you can get is
the maximum eigenvalue

01:15:59.870 --> 01:16:01.340
by the minimum eigenvalue.

01:16:01.340 --> 01:16:02.840
And KLT it gives you that.

01:16:02.840 --> 01:16:05.960
So it's basically saying the n
components which you have gotten

01:16:05.960 --> 01:16:07.460
are the most spread
out they could

01:16:07.460 --> 01:16:09.890
have been in terms of
sigma, which is actually

01:16:09.890 --> 01:16:11.180
something we wanted.

01:16:11.180 --> 01:16:13.520
We started with energy
compaction, and it does that.

01:16:13.520 --> 01:16:17.450
So it's actually giving us
exactly what we wanted to do.

